# [[.Name]]: Heimdall benchmark project

This folder was written by `bifrost-bench init`. It holds everything Heimdall
(bifrost-bench) needs to benchmark your agents on tasks cut from your own
repositories: the gateway stack, your agents, your suites, your experiments
and your release policy. Only the `bifrost-bench` binary and Docker are
needed to use it. Model calls are the only thing that leaves this machine,
and they go through Bifrost.

## Layout

```
deploy/bench.yaml            paths, gateway URL, networks, images
deploy/docker-compose.yml    Bifrost + Postgres + fault proxy
deploy/.env.example          copy to deploy/.env and fill in (git-ignored)
deploy/Dockerfile            the bench image the fault proxy runs
deploy/helper/Dockerfile     the trusted helper image (git, Python, pytest)
agents/                      one YAML file per agent; oracle, noop, refuse,
                             exploit and toy are the free control agents
benchmarks/<suite>/          suite.yaml, base/Dockerfile, tasks/<task>/
experiments/                 experiments/<name>.yaml; smoke.yaml needs no key
policies/default.yaml        the release policy experiments check against
runs/                        sealed run folders (git-ignored)
```

Every command takes `-config deploy/bench.yaml`, which is the default when
you run it from this folder.

## Next commands

Run these from this folder.

1. Fill in the secrets (three random tokens, and a provider key when you
   want real runs; without one the fault proxy answers from its scripted
   model):

       cp deploy/.env.example deploy/.env

2. Build the two local images, the bench image (from a static
   `CGO_ENABLED=0` build of the binary) and the helper image:

       cp "$(command -v bifrost-bench)" deploy/bifrost-bench && docker build -t bench/bifrost-bench:dev deploy && docker build -t bench/coding-core-base:1 deploy/helper

3. Start Bifrost, Postgres and the fault proxy:

       docker compose -f deploy/docker-compose.yml --env-file deploy/.env up -d --wait

   One stack serves every project on a machine: the compose project is
   named `bifrost-bench` and binds fixed ports, so a second `up` from
   another project replaces the running stack instead of starting a new
   one. When a stack already runs here, skip steps 2 and 3: the defaults in
   `deploy/bench.yaml` already point at it (gateway `127.0.0.1:18080`, fault
   proxy `127.0.0.1:19101`, network `bench-evalnet`), so load that stack's
   `.env` instead of writing a new one. Run one project's experiments at a
   time: key slots are leased per process.

4. Create a suite for your language (`python-pytest`, `node-test`,
   `go-test` or `generic-reward`):

       bifrost-bench suite init my-suite -template python-pytest

5. Cut a task from a commit of your own repository that fixes something and
   changes its tests, then rewrite `benchmarks/my-suite/tasks/t01/instruction.md`
   as the request a teammate would send:

       bifrost-bench task init -suite my-suite -id t01 -repo ~/src/app -base <commit before the fix> -reference <fix commit>

6. Check the task, prove it is fit to measure with, then set `suite:` in
   `experiments/smoke.yaml` to `my-suite` and run the free smoke experiment:

       bifrost-bench task validate my-suite/t01 && bifrost-bench task admit my-suite/t01
       set -a; . deploy/.env; set +a; BENCH_SERVER_MODE=synthetic bifrost-bench run experiments/smoke.yaml

Then add your own agent (`bifrost-bench agent init`), write an experiment
that compares two versions of it, and gate releases with
`bifrost-bench check`.
